<section>
  <h2 id="read-a-notification">The relay accepted it. What happened next?</h2>
  <p>A successful send tells you that an SMTP server accepted the message, not that it reached a mailbox.
    Later, a delivery notification may say that one address failed, another is still being tried, and a third received the message.
    Convert that notification into an <code>Email</code> as usual. Simple Java Mail makes the report's fields available without asking your application
    to understand its MIME structure or parse server-specific prose.</p>
  <p>A standards-based delivery notification is itself an email with a machine-readable report alongside the explanation you normally see.
    Simple Java Mail reads those structured fields. It does not guess the result from sentences such as “could not deliver”. A typical notification looks like this:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-text">multipart/report; report-type=delivery-status
├── Human-readable explanation
├── message/delivery-status: recipient actions, status codes and diagnostics
└── Original message or its headers (optional)</code></pre>
  <pre><code class="language-java">Email notification = mail.converter()
    .fromMimeMessage(incomingMessage)
    .toEmail();

DeliveryStatusReport report = notification.getDeliveryStatusReport();
if (report != null) {
    System.out.println(report);
    for (RecipientDeliveryReport recipient : report.getRecipients()) {
        System.out.println(recipient.getAction() + " " + recipient.getStatusCode());
    }
}</code></pre>
  <p>You might see this for a notification containing three recipient reports:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-text">DeliveryStatusReport{recipients=3, delivered=1, delayed=1, failed=1, warnings=0}
FAILED 5.1.1
DELIVERED 2.0.0
DELAYED 4.4.1</code></pre>
  <p>Use the existing EML or MIME conversion methods and, for protected mail, the existing receiving-key inputs.
    There is no separate report converter, parser option or listener. The getter reads an immutable snapshot already extracted during conversion;
    it does not fetch mail or do I/O. Your application still supplies the received message.</p>
</section>
<section>
  <h2 id="correlate-and-decide">Match the notification, then make your decision.</h2>
  <p><code>report.getEnvelopeId()</code> is the reported <code>Original-Envelope-Id</code>. When available, match it to the envelope identifier retained
    from your original <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html">submission receipt</a>. It is not Message-ID, a progress observation ID or a telemetry trace ID.</p>
  <p>Each recipient provides an original and final <code>ReportedRecipient</code>, a <code>DeliveryAction</code>, an
    <code>EnhancedStatusCode</code>, an optional <code>DeliveryDiagnostic</code> and any last-attempt/retry-until timestamps.
    Addresses keep their reported format and spelling, including duplicate occurrences. The reporting server likewise keeps its name and naming format
    together in a <code>ReportedMailServer</code>.</p>
  <p>You don't need to split <code>5.1.1</code> to recognize a permanent failure, or parse a diagnostic's leading SMTP reply number yourself:</p>
  <pre><code class="language-java">for (RecipientDeliveryReport recipient : report.getRecipients()) {
    EnhancedStatusCode status = recipient.getStatusCode();
    if (status != null) {
        System.out.println(status + ": permanent=" + status.isPermanentFailure());
        // For 5.1.1: status class 5, subject 1, detail 1.
    }

    ReportedRecipient target = recipient.getFinalRecipient();
    if (target != null) {
        Recipient mailbox = target.getMailbox();
        if (mailbox != null) {
            // Explicit access to personal data; automatic report rendering omits it.
            System.out.println(mailbox.getAddress());
            // mailbox.getName() is also available when a name was actually reported.
        }
    }

    DeliveryDiagnostic diagnostic = recipient.getDiagnostic();
    if (diagnostic != null &amp;&amp; diagnostic.getSmtpResponse() != null) {
        System.out.println("SMTP reply: " + diagnostic.getSmtpResponse().getReturnCode());
    }
}</code></pre>
  <p>For the failed recipient above, the status line is <code>5.1.1: permanent=true</code>; a reported
    <code>smtp; 550 5.1.1 No such mailbox</code> also exposes SMTP reply <code>550</code>. Subject and detail remain readable numeric components,
    including unfamiliar but well-formed combinations; the API does not require an exhaustive enum of registered codes.</p>
  <p>A <code>ReportedRecipient</code> keeps the source address in <code>getAddress()</code> and its format in <code>getType()</code>.
    Recognized Internet addresses offer a parsed <code>Recipient</code> through <code>getMailbox()</code>. Its name is usually null because a delivery
    report normally contains a mailbox address, not a display name. No To/Cc/Bcc preference or certificate is invented. Foreign formats such as
    <code>x400</code>, malformed mailboxes and address lists keep their reported data but have no mailbox view. Nothing is looked up or contacted.</p>
  <p>The same principle applies to diagnostics: <code>getType()</code> and <code>getText()</code> preserve the reported explanation, while
    <code>getSmtpResponse()</code> is available only for a recognizable SMTP reply. Other protocol formats or explanations without a usable
    reply code remain readable without guessing a number from human prose. That optional view uses the existing <code>SmtpServerResponse</code> API.</p>
  <p>Read the action as well as the status: <code>DELAYED 4.4.1</code> means the reporting system has not finished; a later
    <code>FAILED 4.4.1</code> says it stopped. <code>RELAYED</code> and <code>EXPANDED</code> do not establish final mailbox delivery.
    A reported retry-until time belongs to that system; it is not a schedule for your application to resend.</p>
  <p>Missing or unusable scalar fields are <code>null</code>. Recipient and warning lists are immutable and never null.
    A recognized malformed report retains the usable facts and human-readable warnings; contradictory fields are not silently picked.</p>
</section>
<section>
  <h2 id="imperfect-notifications">Read what you can, even from an imperfect notification.</h2>
  <p>Mail generated by other systems is not always perfectly formatted. Simple Java Mail recovers a clear meaning where it can:
    a status such as <code>5.01.001</code> becomes the numeric view <code>5.1.1</code>, and an explicit ISO timestamp such as
    <code>2026-10-16T10:20:30+02:00</code> can still be read. Each recovery adds a warning; it does not rewrite the original message.</p>
  <p>If the notification's only direct delivery-status part is in the wrong position, it can still be recognized. Two competing parts are different:
    Simple Java Mail leaves the report facts unavailable rather than choosing one. It likewise does not invent a timezone or turn an impossible date
    into another day. Attached or nested notifications remain separate from the outer email's report.</p>
  <p>If optional report reading stops partway through, the completed header blocks remain available and the warnings say extraction may be incomplete.
    An interrupted block is not presented as complete. This does not hide failures to obtain message content: an eager attachment read can still fail
    conversion with its original cause, rather than returning a silently truncated attachment. Check <code>report.getWarnings()</code> before treating
    the extracted facts as a complete account.</p>
</section>
<section>
  <h2 id="trust-and-preservation">Useful facts are not authenticated instructions.</h2>
  <p>A notification can be forged, duplicated, delayed or arrive out of order. Decide which receiving routes and senders your application trusts,
    confirm the correlation, and apply your own idempotency and business rules. Do not suppress a customer address or resend an invoice merely because
    an untrusted email says to. A missing report does not prove delivery, and <code>DELIVERED</code> does not mean somebody read the message.</p>
  <p>Ordinary messages return <code>null</code>. A report attached to an ordinary message is not promoted into its parent's report.
    Existing protection processing runs first: readable signed/decrypted notifications can expose reports, while inaccessible ciphertext cannot.
    Inspect the protection results too; a null report does not establish that encrypted content contains none.</p>
  <p>Copies keep this source snapshot even if you edit their content. New replies and forwards do not inherit it as their own report.
    Exact EML inspection does not rewrite its bytes or add decryption. Outgoing templates cannot manufacture source metadata, and converting a copied
    Email back to MIME does not generate a delivery notification from the snapshot.</p>
  <p>Automatic report rendering includes counts, not addresses, identifiers or diagnostic prose. If you log or export those getters, treat the values
    as untrusted and potentially personal data. JSON/YAML output belongs to your application's normal serializer and DTOs; Simple Java Mail adds no serialization dependency.</p>
</section>
<section>
  <h2 id="teach-the-integration">Connect facts to your application.</h2>
  <p>Simple Java Mail interprets mail. Your job runner, broker or workflow engine owns persistence, scheduling and business transitions.
    See the <a href="/email-workload-field-guide.html#the-invoice-was-accepted-then-bounced">workload field guide</a> for an accepted invoice that later bounces.</p>
  <p>Delivery-status parsing follows <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3464">RFC 3464</a> and
    <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6533">RFC 6533</a>. Human-only bounce messages without a machine-readable report remain ordinary Email content.</p>
</section>
